{"compressedText":"# Prepare a Home for a 72-Hour Power Outage\n\nReady your household to pass safely and comfortably through an outage up to seventy-two hours. Temperate climate, no backup generator. Work sections in order; earlier ones matter most.\n\n## Water\n\nStore four litres per person daily, twelve per person over seventy-two hours. Half for drinking, half for cooking and hygiene. Food-grade containers, out of direct sunlight, replaced every six months. If a storm looms, also fill the bathtub; that water flushes toilets and washes, never drink it.\n\n## Food\n\nStock a three-day shelf of non-perishable food: canned beans, canned fish, peanut butter, crackers, dried fruit, shelf-stable milk. Pick no-cook foods; keep a manual can opener; electric is useless. Eat perishable fridge food first, then freezer, then emergency shelf.\n\n## Fridge/freezer\n\nAn unopened fridge keeps food safe about four hours. A full freezer holds about forty-eight hours, half-full only twenty-four, keep it consolidated and full, even with frozen-water containers. Keep both doors closed; tape a note to each. Discard perishable food over two hours above four degrees Celsius; when in doubt, throw it out.\n\n## Light\n\nUse battery LED lanterns or headlamps for primary light; avoid candles, a leading cause of house fires during outages. Store at least one light per person plus two spare battery sets each. Keep a headlamp by each bed, a lantern in the kitchen, findable in the dark.\n\n## Heat/safety\n\nNever run a camping stove, barbecue, or generator indoors, in a garage, or near a window: each makes carbon monoxide, odourless and fatal. Install at least one battery carbon-monoxide alarm on every floor where people sleep. In cold, pick one small south-facing room, shut its door, gather everyone; layered clothing and blankets beat any improvised heater. If indoor temperature drops below ten degrees Celsius and you cannot stay warm, move to a warming centre or neighbour's home.\n\n## Communication\n\nKeep a battery or hand-crank radio for emergency broadcasts; learn your local emergency station's frequency beforehand. Charge every phone and power bank when a storm looms; a full twenty-thousand-milliamp-hour bank recharges a typical phone about four times. Set your phone to low-power mode at the start; agree beforehand on one out-of-area contact everyone texts; texts beat voice calls on congested networks.\n\n## Medical\n\nKeep a first-aid kit and a seven-day supply of every prescription medication, rotating it to stay in date. Refrigerated medication like insulin stays usable at room temperature up to twenty-eight days, but confirm yours with a pharmacist first. Write your pharmacy, doctor, and poison-control numbers on paper; a dead phone is not a phone book.\n\n## When power returns\n\nWait five minutes before switching major appliances on, letting the grid stabilise. Check the freezer: food holding ice crystals can be refrozen safely. Restock everything used within one week, starting with water and batteries. Write a one-page note on what was missing or awkward, and fix at least one gap before the next outage.","expectedMetric":488,"hardConstraintNote":"Still a self-contained instruction document: retains the title, all eight ordered section headers (Water, Food, Fridge/freezer, Light, Heat/safety, Communication, Medical, When power returns), the intro's scope (temperate climate, no backup generator, work in order), and every actionable step in imperative voice. A reader with only this text can prepare a home for a 72-hour outage end to end -- no external reference is required. No quantity, duration, temperature, item list, or ordered step was removed (GR-3 respected).","killedLeverCitations":["notes/KILLED_LEVERS.md contains no entries (row reads '— none yet'); no K-id fence exists, so this line-editor pass re-proposes nothing killed (GR-6 checked)."],"lens":"line-editor","leverId":"line-edit-baseline-terse-pass","rationale":"Sentence-level compression of the verbose original: converted passive to active (\"should be discarded\"->\"discard\", \"can be safely refrozen\"->kept only where content-bearing), cut throat-clearing (\"This guide explains how to...\", \"it is worth\", \"make sure you own\"), collapsed each restated clause (\"twelve litres per person for the full seventy-two hours\"->\"twelve per person over seventy-two hours\"), stripped redundant connectives (so/since/generally) and non-load-bearing articles, and tightened headers (\"Refrigerator and freezer\"->\"Fridge/freezer\"). Every quantity, duration, temperature, item, and ordered step from the original survives -- I ran an explicit 32-fact self-census before submitting (4L/12L water split; 6 food items; 4h/48h/24h/2h/4C fridge thresholds; light+2 battery-sets; CO/floor/10C heat; 20000mAh/~4x/out-of-area comms; 7-day/28-day/paper-numbers medical; 5-min/ice-crystal/1-week/one-page recovery). HONEST METRIC WARNING: I measured this candidate at 488 by the frontier rule -- ABOVE the current best of 472. The 472 best (r4-compose-line-edit) was a line-edit COMPOSED over the already-folded 526 telegraphic text (two prior composes); a single line-edit over the raw 730 baseline floors ~16 words higher because the only remaining reductions delete semantic content, which my lens forbids. I am therefore reporting the true measured number, not the frontier's optimistic ~450 ceiling estimate (which the frontier itself hedged as \"rough, no recommendation\"). Expected outcome: DOMINATED by best; if assayed, this files as the empirical line-editor floor over the unfolded baseline, and signals that OT-4 (<472) needs a compose-over-folded operation, not a fresh pass over 730.","title":"Single-pass telegraphic line-edit of the 730 baseline"}